.\" auto-reboot(1) - Schedule conditional system reboots via systemd timers
.TH AUTO\-REBOOT 1 "2026-09-11" "1.4.1" "System Administration"
.
.SH NAME
auto\-reboot \- schedule conditional system reboots via systemd timers
.
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B auto\-reboot
.RI [ OPTIONS ]
.
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B auto\-reboot
schedules a system reboot via a systemd transient timer when the system needs
one: pending updates, excessive uptime, or an explicit request. The reboot is
scheduled at the configured time of day using
.BR systemd\-run (1).
.PP
Dry\-run is the default mode. Use
.B \-N
to execute for real.
.PP
Reboot conditions (any one triggers scheduling):
.IP \(bu 2
System updates require a reboot
.RI ( /var/run/reboot\-required " exists)"
.IP \(bu 2
Uptime has reached the maximum threshold (default: 14 days)
.IP \(bu 2
Manual force reboot via
.B \-f
.PP
Only one reboot is pending at a time. When an
.I auto\-reboot\-*.timer
already exists, a new run reports it and schedules nothing, so a nightly cron
entry never stacks timers.
.PP
Root is required only to execute
.RB ( \-N ),
delete timers, or install. Dry runs and
.B \-\-list
work for any user. A member of the
.B sudo
group is re\-executed through
.BR sudo (8)
automatically, with the current settings forwarded as options.
.
.SH OPTIONS
.SS "Execution Mode"
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-dry\-run
Test mode, no execution. This is the default.
.TP
.BR \-N ", " \-\-not\-dry\-run
Execute for real.
.TP
.BR \-f ", " \-\-force\-reboot
Force reboot regardless of conditions. Bypasses the reboot\-required and uptime
checks; time and day restrictions still apply.
.
.SS "Output Control"
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-verbose
Verbose output. This is the default.
.TP
.BR \-q ", " \-\-quiet
Suppress informational messages. Error messages are always shown.
Recommended for cron usage.
.
.SS "Scheduling"
.TP
.BR \-m ", " \-\-max\-uptime\-days " \fIDAYS\fR"
Maximum uptime in days before a reboot is triggered.
Must be a positive integer. Default: 14.
.TP
.BR \-r ", " \-\-reboot\-time " \fIHH:MM\fR"
Time to schedule the reboot in 24\-hour format.
Valid range: 00:00 to 23:59. Default: 22:00.
.TP
.BR \-a ", " \-\-allowed\-days " \fIDAYS\fR"
Restrict reboots to specific days of the week. Accepts a comma\-separated
list of day names or numbers. See
.B DAY SPECIFICATIONS
below. When omitted, any day is allowed.
.
.SS "Schedule Management"
.TP
.BR \-l ", " \-\-list
List all active auto\-reboot timers.
.TP
.BR \-d ", " \-\-delete " \fITIMER\fR"
Delete a specific auto\-reboot timer immediately.
.I TIMER
can be the full name
.RI ( auto\-reboot\-1234.timer ),
partial name
.RI ( auto\-reboot\-1234 ),
or just the numeric ID
.RI ( 1234 ).
.TP
.BR \-D ", " \-\-delete\-all
Delete all auto\-reboot timers. In dry\-run mode (the default) it only lists
what would be deleted. With
.B \-N
given first, it asks for confirmation on the terminal and then deletes;
without a terminal it refuses.
.
.SS "Other"
.TP
.BR \-i ", " \-\-install
Install a root\-owned copy of the script to
.IR PREFIX /bin
(default
.IR /usr/local/bin ).
Use
.B make install
from the source directory to install the manpage and bash completion as well.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Print version and exit.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print help text and exit.
.PP
Short options can be bundled:
.B \-Nqf
is equivalent to
.BR "\-N \-q \-f" .
Standalone operations
.RB ( \-l ", " \-d ", " \-D ", " \-i ", " \-V ", " \-h )
run as soon as they are parsed, so
.B \-N
must come before
.BR \-D .
.
.SH DAY SPECIFICATIONS
The
.B \-\-allowed\-days
option accepts comma\-separated values in any of these formats:
.PP
.TS
l l.
Short names	Sun, Mon, Tue, Wed, Thu, Fri, Sat
Full names	Sunday, Monday, \&..., Saturday
Numbers	0 (Sunday) through 6 (Saturday)
Mixed	Mon,Wed,5
.TE
.PP
Case insensitive. Whitespace around commas is ignored.
.
.SH CONFIGURATION
Site defaults are read from
.IR /etc/auto\-reboot.conf ,
which is sourced as bash. The file must be owned by root and must not be
group\- or world\-writable; otherwise it is refused (exit status 13). A file
that fails to load is fatal (exit status 19). No other path is searched.
Command\-line options override every value.
.TP
.B MACHINE_REBOOT_TIME
Reboot time in HH:MM format. Default: 22:00.
Overridden by
.BR \-\-reboot\-time .
.TP
.B MACHINE_UPTIME_MAXDAYS
Maximum uptime in days. Default: 14.
Overridden by
.BR \-\-max\-uptime\-days .
.TP
.B MACHINE_ALLOWED_DAYS
Day list in the formats accepted by
.BR \-\-allowed\-days .
Default: empty (any day).
Overridden by
.BR \-\-allowed\-days ;
an empty argument clears the restriction.
.PP
Values are validated at startup (exit status 19 on a bad value). The
.BR sudo (8)
re\-execution reads the same file, so settings apply to sudo\-group members
as well as to root.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B PREFIX
Installation prefix for
.BR \-\-install .
Default: /usr/local.
.
.SH SCHEDULING LOGIC
.IP 1. 3
.B No day restrictions:
Schedules for today at the specified time, or tomorrow if that time has
already passed.
.IP 2. 3
.B With day restrictions:
Scans up to 7 days ahead for the next allowed day at the specified time.
.IP 3. 3
.B Force reboot:
Bypasses the reboot\-required and uptime checks. Time and day restrictions
still apply.
.IP 4. 3
.B One pending reboot:
An existing auto\-reboot timer is reported and left alone.
.PP
Timers are created as systemd transient units named
.IR auto\-reboot\-EPOCH .timer
using
.BR systemd\-run (1)
with
.BR AccuracySec=1s .
.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
Runtime failure (systemd not running, scheduling failed, cannot prompt for
confirmation).
.TP
.B 2
Unexpected positional argument.
.TP
.B 13
Not root and not a member of the sudo group; or the configuration file is not
owned by root or is writable by group or others.
.TP
.B 18
.BR systemd\-run (1)
not found.
.TP
.B 19
Configuration file failed to load, or holds an invalid value.
.TP
.B 22
Invalid option or option value (bad time format, invalid day, missing option
value).
.
.SH EXAMPLES
Check whether a reboot is needed (dry run, no root required):
.PP
.RS
.nf
auto\-reboot
.fi
.RE
.PP
Schedule the reboot if conditions are met:
.PP
.RS
.nf
auto\-reboot \-N
.fi
.RE
.PP
Force a reboot at 03:00, quietly:
.PP
.RS
.nf
auto\-reboot \-Nqf \-r 03:00
.fi
.RE
.PP
Restrict to weekends only:
.PP
.RS
.nf
auto\-reboot \-a Sat,Sun \-r 04:00 \-N
.fi
.RE
.PP
List, preview a delete\-all, then delete with confirmation:
.PP
.RS
.nf
auto\-reboot \-\-list
auto\-reboot \-\-delete 1753063354
auto\-reboot \-D
auto\-reboot \-N \-D
.fi
.RE
.
.SS "Cron Integration"
.PP
.RS
.nf
# Nightly check, reboot Sunday at 04:00 once uptime reaches 14 days
0 23 * * * /usr/local/bin/auto\-reboot \-q \-m 14 \-r 04:00 \-a Sun \-N

# Check every 6 hours, reboot at 22:00 once uptime reaches 30 days
0 */6 * * * /usr/local/bin/auto\-reboot \-q \-m 30 \-N

# Weekly forced reboot Sunday at 03:00
0 2 * * 0 /usr/local/bin/auto\-reboot \-qf \-r 03:00 \-N
.fi
.RE
.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/auto\-reboot.conf
Site defaults; see
.BR CONFIGURATION .
Installed once by
.B make install
with default values and never overwritten.
.TP
.I /var/run/reboot\-required
Presence triggers a reboot. Written by
.I /usr/share/update\-notifier/notify\-reboot\-required
(package
.BR update\-notifier\-common )
when kernel or library updates require a restart.
.TP
.I /usr/local/bin/auto\-reboot
Default installation location.
.
.SH LOGGING
Schedule and delete operations are logged to syslog via
.BR logger (1)
with tag
.BR auto\-reboot .
View logs with:
.PP
.RS
.nf
journalctl \-t auto\-reboot
.fi
.RE
.
.SH DEPENDENCIES
.TP
.BR systemd\-run "(1), " systemctl (1)
Part of the
.B systemd
package on every systemd\-managed host.
.TP
.B update\-notifier\-common
Provides the hook that creates
.IR /var/run/reboot\-required .
Without it only the uptime threshold can trigger a reboot.
.B make install
installs the package when the hook is missing.
.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR systemd\-run (1),
.BR systemctl (1),
.BR shutdown (8),
.BR sudo (8),
.BR logger (1)
.
